{"tb-503-1":{"number":"1","question":"<p><i><b>Current nuclear power plants make use of nuclear </b></i><i><b>fission to generate electricity. Which of the </b></i><i><b>following statements about the fission reaction </b></i><i><b>used is/are correct?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) It is a kind of spontaneous reaction.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) The electrostatic repulsion between nuclei </b></i><i><b>must be overcome for this reaction to start.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) The mass of fission fuel should exceed a </b></i><i><b>certain value to sustain a chain reaction.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (1) and (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (2) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>A</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Spontaneous fission reaction occurs at a very low rate. In nuclear power plants, nuclear fission is mainly triggered by bombardment of neutrons. </p><p>  ∴ (1) is incorrect. </p><p>  Neutron is neutral in charge, hence needs not overcome electrostatic repulsion to get close to a nucleus.</p><p>  ∴ (2) is incorrect. </p><p>  The mass of fissile material must exceed the critical mass to sustain chain reaction.</p><p>  ∴ (3) is correct. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":4},"id":"tb-503-1","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-2":{"number":"2","question":"<p><i><b>The mass of a water molecule is 2.99 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>−26</sup></b></i><i><b> kg. </b></i><i><b>This mass is equivalent to an energy of</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A 1.35 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>−9</sup></b></i><i><b> J.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B 5.38 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>−9</sup></b></i><i><b> J.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 8410 MeV.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D 16 800 MeV.</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Energy</p><p>  = (2.99 × 10<sup>−</sup><sup>26</sup>) × (3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup> </p><p>  = 2.69 × 10<sup>−</sup><sup>9</sup> J</p><p>  =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2.69 × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>× 1.6 × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−19</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> MeV</p><p>  = 16 800 MeV</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":20},"id":"tb-503-2","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-3":{"number":"3","question":"<p><i><b>Which of the following conditions is/are necessary </b></i><i><b>to sustain a chain reaction in the fission of </b></i><i><b>uranium?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) The temperature is over 10</b></i><i><b><sup>7</sup></b></i><i><b> K.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) More than one neutron on average is released </b></i><i><b>in each fission reaction.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) All neutrons emitted in a fission reaction will </b></i><i><b>take part in another fission reaction.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (1), (2) and (3)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  A high temperature is not required for fission reaction to occur. </p><p>  ∴ (1) is incorrect. </p><p>  Some neutrons may escape without triggering further fission. A chain reaction is sustained if at least one neutron on average from each fission causes another fission. </p><p>  ∴ (2) is correct. (3) is incorrect. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":32},"id":"tb-503-3","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-4":{"number":"4","question":"<p><i><b>Among the nuclear reactions represented by the </b></i><i><b>following equations, which is/are nuclear fusion?</b></i></p><p>(1) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Be</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p>(2) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Li</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p>(3) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Li</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Be</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>B (2) only</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (1) and (3) only </b></i></p><p><i><b>D (2) and (3) only</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p>  (1) and (3) are fusion reactions.</p><p>  (2) is a fission reaction.</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":46},"id":"tb-503-4","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-5":{"number":"5","question":"<p><i><b>Arrange the following amounts of energy in </b></i><i><b>ascending order.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1) 1 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>25</sup></b></i><i><b> eV</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(2) 1 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>6</sup></b></i><i><b> J</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3) 1 kW h</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A (1), (3), (2)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B (1), (2), (3)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C (2), (1), (3)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D (2), (3), (1)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>C</p>","reasoning":"<p>  1 × 10<sup>2</sup><sup>5</sup> eV</p><p>  = (1 × 10<sup>2</sup><sup>5</sup>) × (1.6 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>19</sup>) J</p><p>  = 1.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> J</p><p>  1 kW h</p><p>  = 1 × 1000 × 3600 J</p><p>  = 3.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> J</p><p>  ∴ 1 × 10<sup>6</sup> J &lt; 1 × 10<sup>2</sup><sup>5</sup> eV &lt; 1 kW h</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":58},"id":"tb-503-5","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-6":{"number":"6","question":"<p><i><b>A nuclear reaction is represented by the following </b></i><i><b>equation:</b></i></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>The mass of a helium-4 nucleus is 4.001 505 u  </b></i><i><b>and that of a neutron is 1.008 665 u. If </b></i><i><b>1.82 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>−12</sup></b></i><i><b> J of energy is released during the </b></i><i><b>reaction, which of the following gives the best </b></i><i><b>estimation of the mass of a hydrogen-3 nucleus?</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A 2.4990 u</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B 2.5112 u</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C 3.0033 u</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D 3.0155 u</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>D</p>","reasoning":"<p>  Δ<i>m</i> =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.82 × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−12</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3 × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>× (1.661 × </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>10</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−27</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>= 0.012 175 u</p><p>  Let <i>M</i> be the mass of a hydrogen-3 nucleus. </p><p>    Δ<i>m</i>  = 2<i>M</i> – (4.001 505 + 2 × 1.008 665) </p><p>    0.012 175  = 2<i>M</i> – 6.018 835</p><p>    <i>M</i>  = 3.0155 u</p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":75},"id":"tb-503-6","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-7":{"number":"7","question":"<p><i><b>Uranium-235 only makes up 0.7% of the natural </b></i><i><b>uranium. The concentration of uranium-235 is </b></i><i><b>enriched to above 3% in the fuel used in nuclear </b></i><i><b>power plants. Having a higher concentration of </b></i><i><b>uranium-235,</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>A </b></i><i><b>neutrons in the fuel travel at a higher speed.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>B </b></i><i><b>a neutron is more likely to trigger further </b></i><i><b>fission when being released.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>C more neutrons are produced when a </b></i><i><b>uranium-235 nucleus undergoes fission.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>D more energy is released when a uranium-235 </b></i><i><b>nucleus undergoes fission.</b></i></p>","answer":"<p>B</p>","reasoning":"<p>  The speed of neutrons, energy released and number of neutrons produced do not depend on the concentration of uranium-235.</p><p>  ∴ A, C and D are incorrect. </p><p>  A neutron is more likely to trigger further fission when being released if there are more fissile nuclei nearby. </p><p>  ∴ B is correct. </p>","kind":"multiple-choice","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":89},"id":"tb-503-7","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-9":{"number":"9","question":"<p><i><b>In nuclear fusion, two hydrogen-2 nuclei are </b></i><i><b>brought close to each other at high speeds </b></i><i><b>(Fig a). A helium-3 nucleus is formed.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/d67ba4c7d3520096b6974cd9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:140.8px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) State the meaning of nuclear fusion. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Write down an equation for the above fusion </b></i><i><b>reaction. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) Explain why the hydrogen-2 nuclei should </b></i><i><b>be brought close to each other at high </b></i><i><b>speeds. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  In nuclear fusion, two nuclei join together to form a heavier nucleus and other particles.  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mtext>e </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow></math>  2A</p><p>  <b>(c)</b>  Since nuclei carry positive charge, they repel each other.  1A</p><p>    The nuclei move at a high speed to overcome the repulsion between them.  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":100},"id":"tb-503-9","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-10":{"number":"10","question":"<p><i><b>The mass of the Sun (Fig b) is 1.99 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>30</sup></b></i><i><b> kg. It </b></i><i><b>radiates energy at a power of 3.90 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>26</sup></b></i><i><b> W.</b></i></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-exercises/media/b2eceb65d9e2a0854d0b8977.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:159.9px\"/></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) What kind of nuclear reaction is responsible </b></i><i><b>for the large amount of energy radiated by </b></i><i><b>the Sun? </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) About 0.07% of the mass of the Sun is </b></i><i><b>converted into energy in a certain period of </b></i><i><b>time. Find the length of the time period in </b></i><i><b>years. Given: 1 year = 3.16 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>7</sup></b></i><i><b> s. </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  Nuclear fusion  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  Mass converted into energy</p><p>    = 1.99 × 10<sup>30</sup> × 0.07%</p><p>    = 1.393 × 10<sup>27</sup> kg</p><p>    Energy released</p><p>    = (1.393 × 10<sup>27</sup>) × (3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup>   1M</p><p>    = 1.2537 × 10<sup>44</sup> J</p><p>    Length of the time period </p><p>    =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Energyreleased</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Power</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>    =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.2537×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>44</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3.90×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>26</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>    = 3.2146 × 10<sup>17</sup> s</p><p>    = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3.2146×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>17</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3.16×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>yr</p><p>    = 1.02 × 10<sup>10</sup> yr  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":111},"id":"tb-503-10","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-11":{"number":"11","question":"<p><i><b>Assume that a nuclear fusion reactor is </b></i><i><b>constructed. A series of nuclear reactions </b></i><i><b>occur to generate energy. The net result of the </b></i><i><b>reactions is described by the following equation:</b></i></p><p>3<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p>Given: mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 2.013 553 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 4.001 505 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 1.007 276 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 1.008 665 u</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Find the total amount of energy (in J) </b></i><i><b>released in the series of reactions. (3 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) The amount of electrical energy consumed </b></i><i><b>by Hong Kong in one day is about </b></i><i><b>4.5 </b></i><i><b>×</b></i><i><b> 10</b></i><i><b><sup>14</sup></b></i><i><b> J. If this reactor were used as the </b></i><i><b>only energy source for Hong Kong, estimate </b></i><i><b>the number of hydrogen-2 atoms consumed </b></i><i><b>every day. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b></b>  <b>(a)</b>  Δ<i>m</i>  = (3 × 2.013 553) – (4.001 505 <br/>    + 1.007 276 + 1.008 665)  1M</p><p>      = 0.023 213 u</p><p>    Energy released</p><p>    = 0.023 213 × (931 × 10<sup>6</sup>) × (1.6 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>19</sup>)<br/>  1M</p><p>    = 3.46 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>12</sup> J  1A</p><p>  <b>(b)</b>  Number of hydrogen-2 atoms consumed every day</p><p>    = 3 ×<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4.5×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>14</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3.46×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−12</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>    = 3.90 × 10<sup>26</sup>   1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":130},"id":"tb-503-11","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-12":{"number":"12","question":"<p><i><b>In March 2011, a nuclear accident happened </b></i><i><b>at the Fukushima Nuclear Power Station in </b></i><i><b>Japan. Some radioactive substances leaked </b></i><i><b>into the surrounding areas. One of the leaking </b></i><i><b>substances was iodine-131. It is produced from </b></i><i><b>the following reaction:</b></i></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>92</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>235</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>53</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>131</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>39</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>102</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + x<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Find the value of </b></i><i><b>x</b></i><i><b>. </b></i><i><b>(1 mark)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Explain the importance of the neutron(s) </b></i><i><b>produced in the reaction for a nuclear power </b></i><i><b>station. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p>(c) Given: mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>92</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>235</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 234.993 422 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>53</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>131</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 130.877 029 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>39</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>102</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 101.912 917 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 1.008 665 u</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>Find the energy released in the reaction in </b></i><i><b>MeV. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  Consider the mass numbers.</p><p>      235 + 1  = 131 + 102 + 1 × <i>x</i> </p><p>      <i>x</i>  = 3  1A</p><p>  <b>(</b><b>b</b><b>)</b>  The neutrons emitted in nuclear fission may collide with other U-235 nuclei and trigger further fissions.   1A</p><p>    Therefore, fission reaction carries on and leads to chain reaction.  1A</p><p>  <b>(c)</b>  Δ<i>m</i>  = (234.993 422 + 1.008 665) <br/>    – (130.877 029 + 101.912 917 + <br/>    3 × 1.008 665)  1M</p><p>      = 0.186 146 u</p><p>    Energy released</p><p>    = 0.186 146 × 931</p><p>    = 173 MeV  1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":148},"id":"tb-503-12","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-13":{"number":"13","question":"<p><i><b>The following fission reaction occurs in a </b></i><i><b>nuclear reactor:</b></i></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>92</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>235</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Xe</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>54</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>140</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Sr</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>38</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>94</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> + 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math></p><p>Given: mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>92</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>235</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 234.993 422 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Xe</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>54</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>140</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 139.892 009 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Sr</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>38</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>94</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 93.894 494 u</p><p>mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math> = 1.008 665 u</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) Find the energy (in J) released in this </b></i><i><b>reaction. </b></i><i><b>(3 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) Describe how a chain reaction is formed in </b></i><i><b>the reactor. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(c) If a power of 1200 MW is delivered </b></i><i><b>by the nuclear fuels in the reactor and </b></i><i><b>the uranium-235 nuclei only undergo </b></i><i><b>the above reaction, find the number of </b></i><i><b>uranium-235 nuclei consumed every second. </b></i><i><b>(1 MW = 10</b></i><i><b><sup>6</sup></b></i><i><b> W) </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p>","answer":"<p><b></b><b></b><b>  </b><b>(a)</b>  Δ<i>m</i>  = (234.993 422 + 1.008 665) – <br/>    (139.892 009 + 93.894 494 + <br/>    2 × 1.008 665)  1M</p><p>      = 0.198 254 u</p><p>    Energy released</p><p>    = 0.198 254 × (931 × 10<sup>6</sup>) × (1.6 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>19</sup>)<br/>  1M</p><p>    = 2.953 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>1</sup><sup>1</sup> J</p><p>    ≈ 2.95 × 10<sup>–</sup><sup>1</sup><sup>1</sup> J  1A</p><p>  <b>(</b><b>b</b><b>)</b>  According to the equation, 2 neutrons are emitted in each fission. Some of these neutrons would collide with other U-235 nuclei trigger further fissions.   1A</p><p>    Therefore, fission reaction carries on and leads to chain reaction.  1A</p><p>  <b>(c)</b>  Energy released every second</p><p>    = <i>Pt</i></p><p>    = (1200 × 10<sup>6</sup>) × 1</p><p>    = 1.2 × 10<sup>9</sup> J</p><p>    Number of uranium-235 nuclei consumed every second </p><p>    =<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1.2×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2.953×1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−11</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></math>  1M</p><p>    = 4.06 × 10<sup>19</sup>   1A</p>","reasoning":"","kind":"structured","source":{"name":"503 Revision 3 Nuclear Energy.docx","sha256":"d267a13f1712b9ee834089d5777527dffcdc4c52d966df657df5bf6366d54643","block":169},"id":"tb-503-13","chapter":"503"},"tb-503-14":{"number":"14","question":"<p>A polonium-210 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Po</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mprescripts/><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>84</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>210</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></math>) nucleus releases 5.4 MeV of energy by emitting an α particle and decays to a stable lead (Pb) nucleus. The half-life of polonium-210 is 138 days.</p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(a) The mass of an </b></i><i><b>α</b></i><i><b> particle is 4.0015 u. </b></i><i><b>Estimate the speed of the </b></i><i><b>α</b></i><i><b> particle emitted </b></i><i><b>in the decay. You may assume that all energy </b></i><i><b>released in the decay becomes the kinetic </b></i><i><b>energy of the </b></i><i><b>α</b></i><i><b> particle. </b></i><i><b>(2 marks)</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(b) There are 10</b></i><i><b><sup>10</sup></b></i><i><b> polonium-210 nuclei in a </b></i><i><b>sample.</b></i></p><p><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b> </b></i><i><b>(i) F